- F-57 **A numeral's type depends on the path and on the library in scope, and in generic code only the getters `x.zero` and `x.one` and the witness `typecase` give a `T` on both paths.** The witness `typecase` is a `typecase` over a function of type `() -> T`, with one clause for each number type (P-78). The checker types a numeral `IntLiteral` or `FloatLiteral` by its form, as the builtin library in scope declares those types (F-79). Walk types an integer numeral by its magnitude, as a `ZZ32`, `ZZ64` or `ZZ`, which it prints by those names (F-328). In code generic over `T extends Integral[\T\]`, `x + 1` fails the checker but runs under walk: no coercion from a numeral into an arbitrary such `T` can be declared, since a generic coercion is refused as a cyclic hierarchy. A numeral bound to `NN32` or `NN64` fails under walk, and `1.5` bound to `RR32` fails on both paths (row 454). Neither path refuses an out-of-range numeral statically: the compiled run raises `Not in range for ZZ32` at run time, and walk refuses it when it runs, at the typed binding it does not fit (row 325). Walk also refuses, against the specification, a numeral beyond `ZZ32`'s range bound to `RR64`, which should hold the float (row 443). Detail: `perf-probes/prelude/distance-triage.md` section 2.3; `reviews/numerics-plan-coordinator/evidence-B.md` sections 3.1, 4.1 and 6.2, measured before the numeral switch (F-79); `x.zero` by reading, not measured.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-any-carrier/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: a2f0b5cf2; note: walk's ilk prints the trait names since rung A of climb batch 27, 5ae581c4a; before, it printed Int, Long and BigNum

- F-308 **A `builtinPrimitive` binding over `NN32`, `NN64` or `Char` whose helper takes or answers a Java `int` or `long` compiles on the compiled path through the six transfer helpers, and `NN32`'s 30, `NN64`'s 27, `UnsignedLong`'s 3 and `Char`'s 40 bindings name their helpers.** Where the declaration says `NN32`, `NN64` or `Char`, or an argument `UnsignedLong`, walk's object of `NN64` (F-313), and the helper has an `int` or a `long`, the rewrite of F-298 wraps that argument, or the call, in the matching transfer, as the compiler's prelude does; walk needs none. A helper overloaded at its arity gets no transfer (row 885), nor does `self` in a generic owner; the declared type is read by its simple name (row 886). The transfers are identities, so the checker passes whatever the helper computes: a binding over `NN32` to the signed `simpleIntArith.intLT` compiles and answers `true` for 2^31 < 1, as walk does; only the walk and compiled guards check that each of them but `Char`'s `asExprString` and F-327's six shifts and powers, which its own tests check, names the unsigned helper. Under walk those 93 keep the glue classes' values, but `NN32`'s and `NN64`'s `DIV`, `REM` and `MOD` by zero throw `DivisionByZero`. All 100 bindings over these types name helpers, `UnsignedLong`'s `asFloat` at `RR64` among them; `Char`'s `asExprString` names `simpleChar.charToExprString` (F-329). The `fortress-repo` skill's `references/compiler.md`, "Natives", names the transfers. Test: `compiler_tests/NativeHelperTransferCompiled`; the guards `NativeHelperFamilyNN32Compiled`, `NativeHelperFamilyNN64Compiled`, `NativeHelperFamilyCharCompiled`, `NativeHelperFamilyAsFloatCompiled`, `NativeHelperFamilyUnsignedCompiled` and `tests/NativeHelperFamilyNN32Walk`, `NativeHelperFamilyUnsignedWalk`, `NativeHelperFamilyNN64Walk`, `NativeHelperFamilyCharWalk`; `tests/NN32DivisionByZeroWalk`, `NN64DivisionByZeroWalk`. Detail: `compile-ladder/climb-batch-27/RECORD.md`, "The count of the bindings"; `compile-ladder/rung-natives-hard/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why"; `compile-ladder/rung-natives-hard/SKEPTIC.md`, "The failure mode".
  from: compile-ladder/climb-batch-27/RECORD.md, "The count of the bindings"; from: compile-ladder/rung-natives-shift-power/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: a2f0b5cf2; note: Char's asExprString moved onto its helper by rung C of climb batch 27, fc860b655 (C +1 in the gather's count); row 861 closed with --settled; rung I, 0122a399a, added three bare bindings each at NN32 and NN64, through F-308's transfers (its report, "What changed and why"), so 27 and 24 became 30 and 27, and the 94 became 100

- F-131 **The one library's number tower is flat.** It builds P-43. `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `NN32`, `NN64`, `ZZ`, `QQ`, `RR64`, `RR32` and `IntLiteral` are siblings under `Number`. `IntLiteral` is the type that the library declares for an integer numeral. The compiled checker gives a numeral that type; walk still gives it `ZZ32`, or `ZZ64` or `ZZ` when its value does not fit (P-76). Each wider integer type converts from each narrower one, and `RR64` from a `ZZ32` and an `IntLiteral`; a `ZZ64` goes into `RR64` only when written out (P-74). `QQ` converts from the five integer types and `IntLiteral`, and `RR64` converts from `RR32` too (F-139). `NN32` does not yet convert into `RR64`: P-75 decides that it will. `Number` declares no `=`; each type declares one at each type it coerces from or into (F-324). Under walk, a big operator over a list of numerals, with no static argument on either, such as `SUM <|1, 2, 3|>`, finds no matching overload at run time (row 432), and an empty `SUM` over an additive group that is not a number dies (row 436). Gated by `tests/FlatTowerRungF.fss` for every type but `RR32` and `IntLiteral`: that none is another's subtype, their coercions and `=`. `RR32`'s place is gated with F-139, and `IntLiteral`'s with F-79. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-flat-tower/REPORT.md` sections 5 and 17.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-number-open/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: a2f0b5cf2; note: Number's = struck by rung N of climb batch 27, a97d60dc6; row 434 closed as FIXED

- F-151 **In the interpreter's library, `Vector`, `Matrix` and their factories and operators bound their element type by `T extends { Number, MultiplicativeRing[\T\] }`.** `Number` declares no arithmetic, so the compiled checker accepts the bodies' arithmetic only through `MultiplicativeRing[\T\]`. `Number` stays in the bound, though it has no `comprises` clause now (F-324). The four juxtapositions of a vector or a matrix with a scalar `T`, in either order, stand beside the library's juxtapositions with a `String`, and the checker finds no error in them (F-53); no run measures what now keeps a scalar `T` apart from `String`. The vector norm is declared at each of the eight number types, by its own `asFloat` (F-324). The eight number types meet the bound. `Number` itself and the numeral type `IntLiteral` do not, since neither is a ring at its own type. So `Vector[\Number,n\]` is not a well-formed type, and `array1` and `array2` have no branch for `Number`: called at `Number`, they give a plain array at run time, not a vector or a matrix (F-229). The checker accepts a call whose argument's type is built from the caller's own type parameter under the same bound, such as the norm's `squaredNorm(me)` (F-227). The scalar extension, the eight operators between an array and a scalar, has bounds of its own, also with `Number` (F-119). Gated under walk by `tests/ArrayElementAlgebra.fss`. The bound is the default that `reviews/array-fork2-judgement.md` recommended. It landed when the curator said to land it. Detail: that judgement, sections 2.5 (what `Number`'s `comprises` clause gives), 2.9, way 3 (the ring alone, measured) and 4 (the bound and its cost).
  from: compile-ladder/rung-number-open/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: a2f0b5cf2; note: Number's comprises clause and asFloat struck, and the norm declared at each of the eight types, by rung N of climb batch 27, a97d60dc6 (the batch record's fork N8); what now gives the exclusion is not measured

- F-311 **`Int`'s, `RR32`'s, `NN32`'s, `Long`'s and `BigNum`'s `asFloat` answer `RR64`, as each type's own `asFloat` declares in its api (F-324), and name `nativeHelpers`' `intToDouble`, `floatToDouble`, `unsignedIntToDouble`, `longToDouble` and `toDouble`; walk's values are unchanged.** The specification: `numbers.tex:49`. At `Float`, the team's declared result, the checker refused the move ('Function body has type RR64, but declared return type is Float.'); walk carries both in a `double` and answers the same `Float` object (F-303). `longToDouble` and `toDouble` are new, `(double) a` and `a.doubleValue()`. Tests: `tests/NativeHelperFamilyAsFloatWalk`, `compiler_tests/NativeHelperFamilyAsFloatCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-natives-asfloat/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-number-open/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: a2f0b5cf2; note: Number's abstract asFloat (Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:291 at the base) struck by rung N of climb batch 27, a97d60dc6; the bindings' line numbers (LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fss:241, :374, :383, :396, :570 at the time), moved on the merged tree, left to the report

- F-318 **Under walk, the load check reads an own generic beside a functional method of a generic trait on their declared domains, `self` of a self-typed trait read as `C[\X\] & X` in the exclusion test.** It builds P-157 and P-152. Where the parameter-by-parameter check refuses an own generic function beside a functional method of a generic trait that declares no static parameters of its own, walk reads the pair as two own generics (`genericPairValid`), the trait's static parameters standing for the method's own; in `domainsExcludeTogether`'s meet, the self parameter of a trait self-typed directly (its parameter `X` bounded by the trait at `X`, no `comprises` clause or one that lists only `...`) excludes a type where the trait's instance or `X` does (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/interpreter/evaluator/values/OverloadedFunction.java`, `validOnDeclaredDomains`, `traitMethodPairValid`, `selfTypeParameter`). So a program's generic operators between an array of a number type and a number, beside a self-typed additive group's own, load and run as on the compiled path, and with `Number`'s `comprises` clause removed, in a scratch copy before F-324 removed it from the library, walk loads and runs `BooleanOps`, `ArrayScalarExtension`, `ArrayOperatorsBesideLibrary` and `extendNumber`. A trait self-typed through another self-typed trait, and a method with static parameters of its own, are not read so and keep the refusal. Walk applies no extender rule of P-146, so an object that extends a self-typed trait at a type it is not a subtype of now loads and runs one of two fitting declarations (row 893); the checker refuses a generic function more specific than such a method that walk loads (row 894). Gated by `tests/ExclusionVectorScalarWalk`, `ExclusionArrayScalarLibraryShapeWalk`, `GenericBesideTraitMethodInsideWalk` and `GenericInsideTraitMethodWalk` (load and dispatch), and the refusals `ExclusionVectorScalarOverlapWalk` and `GenericInsideTraitMethodReturnRuleWalk`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-vector-scalar/REPORT.md` sections 1, 2 and 4.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-number-open/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: a2f0b5cf2; note: the clause removed from the library by rung N of climb batch 27, a97d60dc6

- F-34 **Under the switch-over's setting the code generator refuses 38 declarations of the fourteen components that the distance stage checks, in 10 kinds, all of them declarations that the checker passes.** The setting is the distance stage's (F-53): the checker's failures caught per declaration, and the bound `Any`. Measured once, on `dbf4825e0`'s code, by `compile-ladder/plan-25/probes/codegen.sh` with its 288 helper strings put back on their glue classes; before local functions compiled, the same probe gave 47 in 11 kinds. The kinds that F-315 removes (`Any` in an `extends` clause, `forbid` and the `test` modifier) and the local function (F-320) no longer occur. A declaration counted at its first wall, and no emitted class was run. Each kind left has a ledger row or a plan line, among them varargs (row 624), the type assumption (row 818, the forms that F-325 lists as refused) and "Not in the trait table" (row 733), so after the switch-over the code generator still stands between the library and bytecode. On climb batch 21b's base (`aa5c67d48`) the dispatch generator refused none of `FortressLibrary`'s 178 overload sets. The count on today's code, with F-325's forms compiled, is not measured. No test checks the 38, since the gate's distance stage runs no code generation. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-codegen-local-functions/REPORT.md`, "5. Programs run old against new; the measure"; `compile-ladder/rung-codegen-walls/REPORT.md`, "5. Programs run old against new, and the probes"; `reviews/phase3-exit/zero-audit.md`, "Part C. The debt carried into phase 4", item 4; `perf-probes/codegen-distance/REPORT.md`, sections 2 to 6.
  from: reviews/batch-27-review.md, "6. The natives and the code generator after phase 4's fifth batch"; commit: a2f0b5cf2; note: row 818 narrowed by rung G of climb batch 27, 8caa399e8; the code generator's count over the fourteen not measured on the merged tree (the post-batch review's Findings); rung T, row 733, did not run

- F-29 **Under walk, the load check refuses a trait or object that extends a closed trait of the program's main component where the trait's `comprises` clause does not allow it.** A closed trait is one with a `comprises` clause. The check reads every trait and object of every component of the program, the library's included, as a possible extender. An extender is allowed in three cases: it lies below a listed type; it is a trait whose own `comprises` clause lists only types that are allowed in turn; or it is a generic trait that some trait or object extends, and each of those extenders lies below a listed type (`Specification/basic/traits.tex`, "Trait Declarations"). The check does not yet read a clause declared outside the main component, the library's among them (row 551), or an object expression's `extends` clause (row 597). Nor does it check that each listed type extends the trait at every instantiation (row 598). A check that read the library's clauses would refuse at load every program that imports the library's `Reflect` (row 595), and three team tests and two demos whose objects extend `Exception` directly (row 596). Their repairs come before that check and are the curator's. Gated by `tests/ComprisesUnlistedExtender` and `ComprisesGenericChildUnlistedExtender`, refused at load, and by the `XXX` tests of rows 487 (a type of another component below a generic subtrait of a closed trait), 551, 597 and 598, which stay green while walk still loads their programs. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-load-check/REPORT.md` section 5.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-number-open/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: a2f0b5cf2; note: row 596 rewritten by rung N of climb batch 27: tests/extendNumber.fss needs no repair, since Number has no comprises clause (P-160, F-324)

- F-123 **A compiled object with no `asString` of its own prints its bare type name, as walk does.** A value whose run-time Java class declares its own `toString`, such as the compiled `RR32`'s, prints by it. Open on the compiled path: through the prelude's `Object`, an object that declares or inherits a zero-argument Fortress member named `toString` overflows the stack when it is printed (row 321), though not through the one library's `Object`, whose `asString` is `self.ilkName`; and `asDebugString` and `asExprString` print differently from walk's, where the specification is silent (row 363). For a string static argument walk is the one that is wrong: it takes the argument from the run-time class, so `Cell("s")` prints `Cell[\FlatString\]`, where the compiled `Cell[\String\]` is the specification's answer (row 364). For a numeral argument neither path gives the specification's type. Gated by `compiler_tests/DefaultRenderRungS`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-default-rendering/REPORT.md`, "Defects and their homes".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-any-carrier/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: a2f0b5cf2; note: row 321 rewritten by rung A of climb batch 27, 5ae581c4a

- P-160 **The one library's `Number` opens: its `comprises` clause goes, so that a program may declare its own number type below `Number`.** The team's compiler prelude, the specification and the team's test `tests/extendNumber.fss` give `Number` no such clause. It went in two steps. First, walk reads the library's scalar `+` and `-` between an array and a number beside `AdditiveGroup`'s own operator, as the compiled checker does (P-152, the exclusion of two generic declarations; row 804, its walk half). Then the clause was removed from `Library/FortressLibrary.fss` and `.fsi` (F-324); the reflective QuickCheck generator got a way to generate a `Number`, `Number`'s `=` and `asFloat` left the trait (P-165), so that a program's own number type compares by its own `=` or by the library's fallback `=` through `SEQV`, and the tests that leaned on the clause were repaired. The clause stayed until the first step landed (F-318): without it, walk refused the library's array operators at load, and with them most programs. `AnyIntegral`'s clause stays (P-44). Why: the clause does no work for the compiled checker, whose count of errors in the one library and distance to the switch-over are 0 with it and without it; and a vector or matrix of a program's own complex numbers needs it gone, since the library bounds `Vector` and `Matrix` by `Number` and `MultiplicativeRing`.
  from: reviews/batch-27-review.md, "Part 4. Routing"; commit: a2f0b5cf2; note: the second step built by rung N of climb batch 27, a97d60dc6; tense brought in line with the tree under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"), named to him in the landing report

- P-163 **`Char`'s `asExprString` writes, for every code point, a text that reads back as the same character: the compiled helper's form, completed.** The helper `nativeHelpers/simpleChar.charToExprString` escapes `"` and `\` as `'\"'` and `'\\'`, writes a printable character as itself with its code, the redundant form `'é 00e9'` that Steele built in 2011, and writes the code alone where the parser cannot read the character: the controls, U+201C, U+201D, U+2028, U+2029 and the surrogates. Walk's binding (`ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fss:665`) names that helper, as P-58 asks of every native binding, so both paths write the same text. The parser tries its longer choices of hexadecimal digits first (`parser/Literal.rats:460-466`), so that a six-digit code reads back. The specification's list of character-literal forms (`Specification/basic/lexical-structure.tex:860-891`) is revised in the revision form (P-96) to name the redundant form, which the parser has read since 2011, with an Appendix I entry. Why: the library's contract, the team's comment "will equal self", holds at every code point only with the code alone or with this form; this form is the team's later word, Steele's helper and the parser rule that he wrote with it in one commit, with his own two escape cases reached; and the peers write a printable character as itself, where the code alone hides it. The compiled path's text changes at about 2K code points and walk's at every code point outside printable ASCII, and no library code, suite test or model line calls a `Char`'s `asExprString`. Detail: `reviews/char-asexprstring-2026-11-06.md`, sections 1, 6 (d), 7 and 8.
  from: reviews/batch-27-review.md, "7. The points the coordinator asked for"; commit: a2f0b5cf2; note: built by rung C of climb batch 27, fc860b655; row 861 closed with --settled, and F-308, F-299 and F-32 rewritten at this landing, so the sentence on them is cut; the two line numbers as on the merged tree (F-329); under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"), named to him in the landing report

- P-165 **`asFloat` and the `=` built on it leave `trait Number`; each built-in number type keeps its own `asFloat`.** `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `NN32`, `NN64`, `ZZ`, `QQ`, `RR32`, `RR64` and `IntLiteral` each declare `asFloat`, so the written conversion `asFloat(x)` into `RR64` is unchanged at every concrete type. Two numbers of different types compare by `=` only where a coercion carries one into the other (P-74); otherwise the program writes the conversion, `asFloat(x) = y`. The vector norm takes a bound that promises a float; its form is the default of a fork on his review list: the norm at each of the eight number types, each by its own `asFloat` (F-324). `Float`'s `^` at a `Number` exponent, `random`, `atan2` and `printThreadInfo` are declared at `RR64`, and coercion carries `ZZ32`, `RR32` and numerals to them. The specification's sentence follows (`Specification/basic-lib/numbers.tex:46-54`). P-74's rule, an exact conversion implicit and a lossy one written, stands. Why: the team's compiler prelude declares `Number` with nothing in it (`LibraryBuiltin/CompilerBuiltin.fss:510`), and the specification does not ground the method: its sentence that names it on `Number` is the revival's revision. The curator's rule for such a case: an early idea of the interpreter's library goes where the specification does not ground it and the later prelude eliminated it. With `Number` open (P-160), an abstract `asFloat` would force a program's own number, such as a complex number, to invent a float, and `Number`'s `=` would compare two different complex numbers by it.
  from: reviews/batch-27-review.md, "The rungs, briefly"; commit: a2f0b5cf2; note: the norm's form is the batch record's fork N8 at its default (coordinator/CLIMB-BATCH-27.md:428, :519), on his review list as review-queue item 393, not his choice; the specification's sentence revised by rung N of climb batch 27, a97d60dc6 (Specification/basic-lib/numbers.tex:46-54, with an Appendix I entry, revival-asfloat), so its clause states the revised text; under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"), named to him in the landing report

- P-166 **`ZZ`'s and `QQ`'s values are value objects, and the specification's list of reference objects stops naming arbitrary-precision numbers.** The library declares `value object BigNum extends ZZ` (`LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fss:565`, `.fsi:223`), so every `ZZ` value is a value object, and `QQ`'s object `Ratio` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:648`) is a `value object` too. By the specification's rule two value objects of one type are strictly equivalent when their fields are, so two `ZZ` values built apart are `SEQV` when their integers are equal, `SEQV` converting each into its bits and comparing those, and two equal rationals built apart are `SEQV` in the same way. The specification's list in `Specification/basic/objects.tex` ("Reference objects include arbitrary-precision numbers") is revised to name them among the value objects, in the revision form (P-96) with one Appendix I entry. Why: the library's `value` declaration is the deliberate word, and where it is, the curator's rule brings the specification's example in line with the code. Detail: `reviews/any-carrier-2026-11-06.md`, section 2.4, point 11, which found the split.
  from: reviews/batch-27-review.md, "The rungs, briefly"; commit: a2f0b5cf2; note: Ratio declared value, and objects.tex:39-63 revised with the Appendix I entry revival-object-kinds, by rung E of climb batch 27, 598331c0d; row 911 closed as FIXED; under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"), named to him in the landing report

- P-167 **The three natives over `Any`: `ilkName` and `SEQV` name the compiled path's helpers, which walk's loader answers with its own operations, and `Object`'s `asString` is written in Fortress as `self.ilkName`.** `ilkName` and `SEQV` keep P-58's form, a bare `builtinPrimitive("…")`, naming `stringOps.typeName` and `equality.sEquiv`. The compiled path runs each helper as it does today, and the helper's answer is the operation's definition. Walk's loader (`interpreter/glue/NativeApp.java`, where it reads a `builtinPrimitive` string) holds a table of the two names and answers each with walk's own operation, as a virtual machine runs its own code for an intrinsic; no implementation is shared, and one test runs on both paths to hold the two together. For these two names P-58's walk half, that walk loads the named helper, bends. `Object`'s `asString` becomes `self.ilkName`, P-91's rule as text, which removes one native and, once the compiled path reads the one library, the compiled stack overflow of row 321. The ilk is the trait's name on both paths, such as `ZZ32` and never walk's `Int`, so walk's native objects stop being types that a program sees or prints. What `SEQV` answers is P-168. Why: the compiled path's answer is the definition and walk is brought in line, the curator's "do it the way the compiler path does it"; the three are the runtime's own operations, not calls into Java helpers. Detail: `reviews/any-carrier-2026-11-06.md`, section 6, options (c) and (e1).
  from: reviews/batch-27-review.md, "The rungs, briefly"; commit: a2f0b5cf2; note: built by rung A of climb batch 27, 5ae581c4a (F-328), so "the rung works out the way" is cut; row 321 now holds the prelude's Object until the switch-over; under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"), named to him in the landing report

- P-168 **What `SEQV` means, on both paths: the specification's rule, with no special case for any type.** Values of different types are never strictly equivalent; two value objects of one type are when their fields are, tuples element by element and floats by their bit patterns; two reference objects are only when they are one object (`Specification/basic/objects.tex`, "Object Equivalence" and "Value Objects"). Both paths read the `value` modifier. Each path changes its own code: walk drops its equivalence across types (row 909) and its answer `true` for value objects whose body declares no field (row 113); the compiled helper `equality.sEquiv` gains the rule for value objects, tuples and floats (rows 906 and 907; the prelude's own `===` keeps row 908 until the switch-over). `ZZ`'s and `QQ`'s values are value objects (P-166). Strings are reference objects: the library declares `FlatString`, `CatString` and `SubString` without `value`, so two such strings are strictly equivalent only when they are one object, and text equality is the strings' own `=`. This overrules the team's code on both paths, walk's `seqv` and the prelude's `opr ===(a:String, b:String) = a=b`, where the specification is silent on strings. A program with a `value object CC64` tests it on both paths beside the review's 14 cases (F-326). Why: the specification's rule read as written, over the team's unfinished code of 2008 and 2010, which its authors called preliminary and incomplete; P-167 makes this one comparison that both paths answer. Detail: `reviews/any-carrier-2026-11-06.md`, section 2.4; `reviews/string-equivalence-2026-11-06.md`, sections 3 and 7.
  from: reviews/batch-27-review.md, "The rungs, briefly"; commit: a2f0b5cf2; note: built by rung E of climb batch 27, 598331c0d: tests/ValueObjectCC64Walk.fss and compiler_tests/StrictEquivalenceRuleCompiled.fss, the compiled CC64 = waiting for the switch-over (the batch record's fork E9); rows 906 and 907 FIXED, row 908 narrowed to the prelude; under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"), named to him in the landing report

